/target/
/result
/result-*
crates/*/target/
crates/schematic-viewer/target/
*.exe
*.dll
*.so
*.dylib
*.tmp
*.bak
.konnect-transaction-*.json
.konnect-transaction-*.abandoned.json
dist/

# Python bytecode. The plugin and packaging scripts are Python; running
# them (or the plugin tests) writes caches next to the sources, and three
# .pyc files had been committed.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]

# Files KiCad writes beside any board it opens. Running the live IPC suite
# means opening a board in KiCad, so every reviewer who does grows a set of
# these, and `git add -A` will otherwise sweep them in — a `.history/` gitlink
# and a lock file naming the developer's host and username both reached a
# commit that way.
#
#   .history/     KiCad 10 Local History: an embedded git repo of automatic
#                 snapshots, per-machine (Preferences > Common > Project Backup)
#   *.lck         transient board lock; contains {hostname, username}
#   *.kicad_prl   per-user local project settings, never shared
#   *.kicad_pro   generated on open for a bare .kicad_pcb fixture
#
# .kicad_pro is ignored in general but deliberately re-included under
# tests/fixtures: the netclass tools (create_netclass, assign_net_to_class)
# write to the sibling project file by design, so a fixture exercising them
# needs a project file tracked on purpose — and a blanket ignore would make
# `git add` skip it in silence, which is the same failure this block exists to
# prevent, only inverted. A *generated* one appearing there should be deleted,
# not committed. .history/, *.lck and *.kicad_prl stay ignored everywhere,
# fixtures included: those must never be tracked even deliberately.
.history/
*.lck
*.kicad_prl
*.kicad_pro
!crates/*/tests/fixtures/*.kicad_pro
